WEBVTT

00:03.960 --> 00:04.720
All right, friends.

00:04.720 --> 00:07.840
So now let's do a quick recap about the window aggregate functions.

00:07.840 --> 00:14.000
So what they do they're going to go and aggregate a set of values and return a single aggregated value

00:14.000 --> 00:14.880
for each row.

00:14.880 --> 00:16.560
So it's very similar to the groupby.

00:16.760 --> 00:18.400
But here we don't lose details.

00:18.600 --> 00:19.480
Now to the next point.

00:19.480 --> 00:22.960
What are the rules for the syntax about the expressions?

00:22.960 --> 00:25.520
They all expect a number in the expression.

00:25.520 --> 00:29.800
So you have to pass a number like sales or any integer, but only for the count.

00:29.800 --> 00:34.280
You can go and use any data type, and the things for the aggregate functions are very simple.

00:34.280 --> 00:38.920
Everything is optional inside the definition of the over clause or the definition of the window.

00:38.920 --> 00:43.360
So you can go and use partition by order, by frames or not, or just leave everything empty.

00:43.360 --> 00:44.960
So everything is optional.

00:44.960 --> 00:48.200
So now as we learned, we have a lot of use cases for the aggregate functions.

00:48.200 --> 00:50.520
And they are really amazing for analytics.

00:50.520 --> 00:55.560
So the first one, the simplest one you can do overall analysis if you just leave the window function

00:55.560 --> 00:56.080
empty.

00:56.240 --> 00:59.520
So you will get one big number about your business and the next use case.

00:59.520 --> 01:02.120
We can do total groups analysis.

01:02.160 --> 01:06.600
As you learned, we can use partition by in order to compare categories with each others, like comparing

01:06.600 --> 01:08.440
the products or customers and so on.

01:08.440 --> 01:11.400
Moving on to the next one, we can do part to whole analysis.

01:11.400 --> 01:15.280
We can go and compare the performance of each data point with the overall.

01:15.280 --> 01:20.950
So you can, for example, compare the sales to the total sales in the window or to the all data sets.

01:20.990 --> 01:22.790
And we have many comparison analyses.

01:22.790 --> 01:27.590
We can go and compare the current value with the average, or we can compare them to the extreme to

01:27.630 --> 01:30.110
the highest sales, to the lowest sales, and so on.

01:30.150 --> 01:31.070
And another use case.

01:31.070 --> 01:34.430
We can go and identify data quality issues in our data.

01:34.510 --> 01:38.430
So we can go, for example, identify duplicates using the count function.

01:38.430 --> 01:40.870
Moving on to the next use case, we have the outlier detection.

01:40.870 --> 01:46.310
We can go and find out which data points are above the average and below the average, and so on.

01:46.550 --> 01:48.750
Then the next one we have the running total.

01:48.790 --> 01:54.390
As we learned, it is a great tool in order to track the progress or to monitor the performance of our

01:54.390 --> 01:56.470
business over the time.

01:56.470 --> 02:02.310
Or if you want to be more specific, you can go and use the rolling total in order to have like a specific

02:02.310 --> 02:06.270
window and only track this window like three months or something like that.

02:06.310 --> 02:10.270
And the last use case, we can go and calculate the moving average of our data.

02:10.270 --> 02:16.270
So it's really amazing how order by and aggregate functions can open for you a door for amazing or advanced

02:16.270 --> 02:16.990
analyses.

02:17.030 --> 02:21.590
So guys, as you can see we have a lot of use cases for the window aggregate functions in the world

02:21.590 --> 02:22.630
of data analytics.

02:22.630 --> 02:23.030
All right.

02:23.030 --> 02:25.710
So with that we have covered the aggregate window functions.

02:25.710 --> 02:27.750
And in the next step it's going to be very important.

02:27.750 --> 02:31.390
We will learn how to rank our data using window functions.

02:31.390 --> 02:32.110
So let's go.
